WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:01.001
(scraping)

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.405
(peaceful music)

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:04.242
- I really enjoy working with fish skin.

00:00:04.242 --> 00:00:06.450
It's something that feels right.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.220
Because I spend so much
time outside, I am inspired

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.470
by this huge landscape that Alaska has.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.430
I tend to gravitate towards
those materials that mimic

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:22.507
the natural beauty that
I'm used to seeing.

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:25.674
(contemplative music)

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:38.551
(wings flapping)

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.637
My name is Joel Isaak.

00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:45.804
I am a Dena'ina Athabaskan
Alaska native artist, and I work

00:00:46.672 --> 00:00:51.359
a lot with living materials
like fish skin, or wood.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:54.080
For thousands of years, people
in Alaska have been fishing,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.040
and only taking what you need
really is a traditional way

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.790
of working with fish.

00:01:00.391 --> 00:01:04.211
I use fish skin to create many
different types of objects.

00:01:04.211 --> 00:01:07.672
I've made high heels for
models; I've made dresses;

00:01:07.672 --> 00:01:10.720
I've made a halibut skin
motorcycle jacket; I make masks.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.237
It's stronger per
thickness than mammal hide,

00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:16.890
and that gives it more strength
for stretching and pulling.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.792
It does expand and contract,
and you can have kind of

00:01:19.792 --> 00:01:21.709
a relationship with it.

00:01:22.811 --> 00:01:25.085
Most the fish skin that I use, I catch.

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.901
And all the fish skin that I
use, I clean, and I process,

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:28.870
and I tan myself.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:30.981
One of the traditional
methods is by fish eggs,

00:01:30.981 --> 00:01:33.891
and that takes three days
of soaking the fish skins,

00:01:33.891 --> 00:01:35.299
and then they need to be hand-softened,

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:36.952
which is a very laborous process.

00:01:36.952 --> 00:01:39.202
(scraping)

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.621
The land has been very
formative for the type of work

00:01:43.621 --> 00:01:46.680
that I create, and fish skin, for me,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.860
is a very sustainable practice.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.141
It's a living material, and
I have a connection to it.

00:01:53.141 --> 00:01:55.520
It's been part of my whole entire life,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.331
being here at the beach.

00:01:57.331 --> 00:02:01.371
That's part of what makes me
Dena'ina is that interaction

00:02:01.371 --> 00:02:05.204
living with salmon and
being around the land.

